---
<Base title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
<Fragment set:html={"<section class=\"article-hero\"><div class=\"shell\"><div class=\"breadcrumb\"><a href=\"/resources/\">Resource Center</a> / <a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Options &amp; Upgrades</a> / 10</div><div class=\"eyebrow\">CUSTOMIZATION TOPIC 10 \u00b7 Storage &amp; Function</div><h1>Lofts, Ladders &amp; Stairs Options</h1><p>Lofts and vertical storage require structural load and headroom planning.</p></div><section class=\"asi-cost-review\" id=\"option-planning-prices\"><h2>Option pricing &amp; budget planning</h2><p class=\"asi-cost-alert\"><strong>Research-stage planning references:</strong> The figures below come from historical ASI planning material or approved model siding formulas. Historical amounts have NOT been verified as current Appalachian Sheds selling prices. They are not quotes. Final prices and any credits for included standard features must appear in a written proposal.</p><table class=\"asi-cost-list\"><thead><tr><th>Option / scope</th><th>Planning figure</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Storage loft platform</td><td>$24\u2013$34</td><td>per loft SF, historical</td></tr><tr><td>Premium loft platform</td><td>$34\u2013$48</td><td>per loft SF, historical</td></tr><tr><td>Simple wooden loft guard</td><td>$90\u2013$140</td><td>per linear foot, historical</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"asi-cost-note\">Standard ASI building features remain included in the approved model price. Figures shown are for planning context only; these are not added to the online shed calculator. No site-specific price should be inferred from a price per linear or square foot.</p><a href=\"/options-gallery/\">Explore visual option comparisons</a></section><section class=\"asi-option-mini-gallery\"><h2>Visual comparison \u2014 planned photographs</h2><p>Images will be added where they help explain actual differences. No placeholder is represented as an installed product or completed ASI project.</p><div class=\"asi-mini-grid\"><div class=\"asi-small-slot\">PHOTO SLOT \u2022 Overall appearance</div><div class=\"asi-small-slot\">PHOTO SLOT \u2022 Close-up detail</div><div class=\"asi-small-slot\">PHOTO SLOT \u2022 Installed scale / context</div></div></section></section><div class=\"shell deep-layout\"><article><section class=\"deep-answer\" id=\"overview\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">THE ESSENTIAL ANSWER</div><h2>What should you know before choosing?</h2><p>A deeper platform adds storage, but it can also reduce light, compress the room below and interfere with doors, windows, workbenches, shelving or electrical planning.</p><p>Loft depth, height and access should be chosen around what will actually be stored and how often the loft will be used.</p></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-1\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 01</div><h2>The Best Loft Is Not Always the Biggest Loft</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>A deeper platform adds storage, but it can also reduce light, compress the room below and interfere with doors, windows, workbenches, shelving or electrical planning.</p><p>Loft depth, height and access should be chosen around what will actually be stored and how often the loft will be used.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Storage Loft Use Is Different From Occupied Loft Use</h3><p>This category is written primarily for storage lofts, workshop overflow and occasional-access upper storage.</p><p>Sleeping, dwelling, rental, bedroom or occupied upper-level use requires separate zoning, building-code, egress, stair and local approval review.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Storage Loft Platforms</h3><p>Storage loft platforms create useful upper-level organization without enlarging the building footprint. Common layouts include compact storage lofts, full-width rear lofts, front-and-rear lofts and workbench-overhead configurations.</p><p>The platform should be sized around the items being stored, the available height and the function of the lower floor.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Lofts Are Common \u2014 Usability Is the Differentiator.</h3><p>Competitors in the Cincinnati market commonly include basic loft area in lofted-barn models. Appalachian Sheds should differentiate through thoughtful sizing, framing, decking, access and integration with the full interior plan.</p><p>Loft over workbench or storage wall</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-2\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 02</div><h2>Loft Decking &amp; Load Upgrades</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Loft decking determines how the platform feels, looks and performs. Standard structural decking works well for ordinary storage, while finished tongue-and-groove decking can improve the visible underside and edge of a premium interior.</p><p>Load upgrades should be driven by actual intended storage rather than by a generic marketing label.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Design for the Stored Weight \u2014 Not Just the Square Footage.</h3><p>Light seasonal bins behave differently from dense tools, equipment, books or concentrated workshop storage.</p><p>Structural plywood or panel decking for storage use</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Railings, Guards &amp; Access Gates</h3><p>Open loft edges and access openings can require protective guards, railings or gates depending on platform height, layout, intended use and local requirements. These should be planned with the loft framing so posts and blocking can be properly integrated.</p><p>Finished railings also become an architectural feature in offices, studios and premium interiors.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Ship Ladders &amp; Compact Access</h3><p>Compact access systems save floor space, but they should be matched to occasional or limited loft use. Carrying large bins or heavy items on a steep access system can quickly become inconvenient.</p><p>Ship ladders and alternating-tread devices also have specific code limitations when they are used as part of required egress or when the upper area is treated as occupied space.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-3\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 03</div><h2>Full Stairs &amp; Premium Access Systems</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Full-run stairs provide the most comfortable and familiar access, but they use the most floor area. They make the most sense when the loft will be accessed frequently, the interior is more finished, or the stair itself is part of the building's architectural plan.</p><p>Stair rise, width, tread and riser geometry, handrails, guards, landings and lower-floor circulation should all be resolved before framing.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Code, Safety &amp; Intended-Use Reality</h3><p>Loft access is not governed by one universal Cincinnati Tri-State rule. Ohio, Kentucky and Indiana use different adopted code editions and local jurisdictions may apply additional permit, zoning or inspection requirements. Final requirements must be confirmed for the project location and intended use.</p><p>For customer planning, Appalachian Sheds should keep the distinction simple: a storage loft with occasional access is different from an occupied, sleeping or dwelling-level loft.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Compare the Loft Systems Before Choosing the Final Layout</h3><p>On smaller screens, scroll horizontally to view the full comparison.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Plan the Loft Before the Building Height and Interior Layout Are Locked In</h3><p>Appalachian Sheds can coordinate platform size, decking, railing, access, lighting, wall height, roof pitch, windows and the lower-floor plan before the final written scope is approved.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"asi-scope\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">IMPORTANT PROJECT DISTINCTION</div><h2>How this relates to an Appalachian Sheds project</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Built on your property</h3><p>All Appalachian Sheds buildings are constructed on site. Property access, drainage, topography, location restrictions and the selected model can affect planning.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Exterior shell versus finished interior</h3><p>Our standard building proposal describes a weather-tight exterior shell. Insulation, electrical, plumbing, heating, cooling, and interior wall or ceiling finishes are separate work unless expressly listed in the written scope.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Only the written scope is authoritative</h3><p>Model-specific specifications, pricing, selected options, finishing requirements and any engineering or permit obligations must be confirmed for the actual building and site. Avoid treating this general guide as an engineering design or a permit determination.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"next\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">MAKE THE NEXT DECISION</div><h2>Your next planning step</h2><p>Sketch equipment and furniture positions, identify required clearances and openings, and compare the result against the available model sizes before finalizing a proposal.</p><div class=\"related\"><a href=\"/buildings/\">View Models &amp; Pricing</a><a href=\"/planning-guides/\">Explore Building Guides</a><a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Browse Options &amp; Upgrades</a></div></section></article><aside class=\"deep-toc\"><strong>On this page</strong><a href=\"#overview\">Essential answer</a><a href=\"#topic-1\">The Best Loft Is Not Always the Biggest Loft</a><a href=\"#topic-2\">Loft Decking &amp; Load Upgrades</a><a href=\"#topic-3\">Full Stairs &amp; Premium Access Systems</a><a href=\"#asi-scope\">How this relates to an ASI project</a><a class=\"btn light\" href=\"/buildings/\">See Your Price</a></aside></div>"} />
</Base>

